e with the evaluation that the  ministers have made it's going to reduce  poverty and is going to increase income  okay we accept that but will it do  anything for equality and this is where  I have a more skeptical and more  critical set of comments I think that it  will do something about inequality of  income but it's much more limited in  terms of whether it will affect the  distribution of power and the  distribution of resources between  families so it's a one legged stool and  what would be a more appropriate  division this the this the second leg of  the stool would be to provide access to  services and here I'm not going to  rehash the well-known arguments about  why we need childcare and affordable  childcare and accessible childcare and  why that has to be of good quality as  well as as existing but the the without  the the addition of the element of  childcare services the redistribution of  income remains limited why because as  this chart tells you if you could read  it is that the distribution of childcare  costs are extremely uneven and childcare  outside of Quebec is extremely expensive  so that the 2300 increase in income will  basically give a family in Toronto two  months of childcare according to the Act  the cost of it so it's it's going to  increase our income it's going to give  them two months but two months is not 12  months two months is not 11 months if we  think about vacations so new services  and do n rights parental leave is not  part of this  this change there's they're beginning to  talk maybe about a father's allowance  and there's nothing about family  allowances and we know from Quebec that  adding in those kinds of benefits of  return 'ti benefits and access to  parental benefits wider access has  increased the sharing of work within the  family or the sharing of parenting  within the family and these have had  real consequences and these these the  differences between Quebec and the other  provinces demonstrate that the addition  of new rights along with new income and  along with new services can change the  inequalities within a society so that we  know these things and therefore my  criticism of the federal government's  policy is that it only has one leg and  not three let's look at another example  this much discussed the example of a  basic income and the prime prime  minister of Quebec has has talked about  it the minister of children families  children Social Development has talked  about having a guaranteed minimum income  does this pass my test is this a  three-legged stool and innovation  obviously it's an instrument for  redistributing income or at the lower  end of the of the ladder but does it do  anything about equality for a basic  income to be a social innovation I and  that would what a social innovation that  would increase social inclusion and  reduce inequalities it would have to be  accompanied by changes in public  services and rights and that these  themselves would have to be seen to be  innovations once again simply changing  the distribution is insufficient it  doesn't make it three-legged what would  these innovations be this is where you  people come in because it involves a new  canoe imagination  imagining new kinds of services and new  kinds of right it involves imagining for  example how to increase the access to  participation and new voices for those  who are who are marginalized and it in  it involves imagining how these this  kind of mixed system of welfare that I  talked about is the lesson from the  European book how that would be brought  about this hat tare foot takes a lot of  experimentation and I'm the academic so  I'm gonna cop out here and I'm gonna say  what I want what I needed is rather than  telling you what to do is I need you to  think about the the ways in which the  mixed regimes of welfare the that the  participation of these sectors can be  combined to provide services services  that promote equality between age groups  how can we include an sure social  inclusion for the young and for the  elderly how to include them in into  democratic life what are the necessary  accompanying services and and what is  the role of each of the actors in this  what would the mixed regime of welfare  look like to break isolation to improve  access to employment what are the rights  that would be necessary in terms of  housing how can that be brought about  and to to work these are just examples  but that's the questions that I think  that that we need to address is how to  do it when we but that with the lesson  them the message I want to leave with  you is that a one legged public policy  is inadequate it depends on those other  two legs and very often those other two  legs come from the kinds of mixed  regimes of welfare that are represented  in the room and therefore I hope in the  discussion we'll be able to think about  how to get those other two legs attached  and move forward thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

